UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 16, Lesson 2: Empathy role plays', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWhy ask whether someone wants ideas or listening first?\n\nFEEDBACK\nHelp should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive.\n\nWATCH FOR\nForcing someone to talk is not empathy.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nRole-play four support scenes. Record which question helped. Pass allowed.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-RRS-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Role-play four support scenes. Record which question helped. Pass allowed. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive. Reasoning: I used empathy by attending to role play rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Forcing someone to talk is not empathy. Correction: Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive. Why: the correction restores accurate use of empathy.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Forcing someone to talk is not empathy. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating empathy on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with empathy, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive. This matches the lesson skill for empathy.","options":["Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive.","Forcing someone to talk is not empathy.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Why ask whether someone wants ideas or listening first?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Forcing someone to talk is not empathy. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive.","Careful use of role play with clear evidence.","Forcing someone to talk is not empathy.","Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named role play, not by copying the model.","options":["Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the role play used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence.","Choose randomly because any option could be right."],"question":"A learner is practising empathy. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive. uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Copy the example and call that independent work.","Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive.","Forcing someone to talk is not empathy.","Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Forcing someone to talk is not empathy.","Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about empathy."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about role play?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?”","Forcing someone to talk is not empathy.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over. — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Forcing someone to talk is not empathy.","Role-play four support scenes. Record which question helped. Pass allowed."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating empathy on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive. Reasoning: I used empathy by attending to role play rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with empathy, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Forcing someone to talk is not empathy. Correction: Help should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive. Why: the correction restores accurate use of empathy.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y4-w16-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise empathy.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Health, Wellbeing and Relationships","success_criteria":["I can use empathy with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify role play and explain why it matters.","I can practise listen independently on a new prompt.","I can notice support needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?”\n\nTRY FIRST\nWhy ask whether someone wants ideas or listening first?\n\nFEEDBACK\nHelp should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive.","explain":"Empathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Forcing someone to talk is not empathy. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"Empathy role plays.","caption":"Match help to need.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Listen</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">paraphrase</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ask</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">ideas or listening</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?” Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?” Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?” Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Support practice.","caption":"Question helped.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Four scenes</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">pass OK</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">force talking</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nForcing someone to talk is not empathy. Here is a more helpful example: Example: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?”\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nHelp should match need; unasked advice can feel dismissive.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Forcing someone to talk is not empathy. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about empathy?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nEmpathy role plays practise listening, naming feelings and offering support without taking over. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 4, Week 16, Lesson 2: Empathy role plays","unit_number":4,"unit_title":"Relationships, communication and respect","week":16,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: listener nods, paraphrases, asks “Do you want ideas or just listening?”\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Empathy role plays.","caption":"Match help to need.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Listen</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">paraphrase</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Ask</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">ideas or listening</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Support practice.","caption":"Question helped.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Four scenes</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">pass OK</text><text x=\"358\" 